    #  Z67. Ruling Z: the shape greps' block also runs the same program over
    #       this script's own source, its four lines and its planted controls,
    #       so a script regenerated after the gate log carries the lines in
    #       the log it writes.
    # CARRIED TO M6 part 1 P1 from M5 part 6 P4's gate script (at the phase
    # commit 9e171da, printed in records/logs/m5p6-p4/gate.log at P4's record
    # commit, which the base holds; its change list A67 to Z67 is above),
    # generated by <home>/.cache/emend-build/m6p1-p1/gen/gate.py, which checks
    # the template byte-equal to that print, replaces each of P4's generated
    # parts whole (each found once) and asserts every other replacement's
    # count, and greps for P4's names, subjects, hashes and roles over the
    # header's paragraph and usage lines and the whole body. What differs,
    # and why:
    #  A68. The phase is Milestone 6 part 1 P1 (specs/0038-chartsheets.md, "P1:
    #       the reader and the writer"): the log directory records/logs/m6p1-p1.
    #       The base is Milestone 5 part 6's close-out record commit ("M5 part
    #       6 close-out record:", 67c9144); the phase holds three commits found
    #       by their full subjects, each beginning "M6 part 1 P1: ": the doc
    #       commit, then the crate commit, then the tool commit, the phase
    #       commit, each the next one's parent and the doc commit's parent the
    #       base. Part 6 P3's ops commit named as either code role, and the two
    #       code commits swapped, are refused by the order check (controls).
    #       The record-commit control of commit_class names the base; the
    #       code-commit control names the commit that last modified
    #       tools/emend-copy/src/main.rs, the tool commit. The count rule reads
    #       all three messages; the doc commit states no count and both code
    #       messages carry the diff-alone sentence.
    #  C68. The prunes (specs/0038 "Phases": P1's gate prunes Milestone 5 part
    #       6's roots): the target root alone of each of <home>/.cache/
    #       emend-build/m5p6-p1, m5p6-p2, m5p6-p3, m5p6-p4 and m5p6-close,
    #       listed with du -sb and removed by rm -r (no -f) under
    #       PRUNE_PREVIOUS=yes, part 6 P3's prune_dir form; a root an earlier
    #       phase removed (part 6 P2's and P3's) is printed "not there"; every
    #       other name of each directory (read when the generator ran) checked
    #       present and accounted for.
    #  D68. The spec is specs/0038, pinned by the hash of its COMMITTED blob at
    #       P1's doc commit (abe3f167..., read from the blob when the generator
    #       ran and checked against the brief's full hash) at the phase commit;
    #       the working tree's copy, planning's (rulings A to C), printed and,
    #       when PLANNING_SPEC is given, checked equal to it; the base holds no
    #       specs/0038; specs/0037 at the phase commit is the control the pin
    #       refuses. The doc commit's three files (specs/0038 added, specs/0037
    #       with the close-out verification and rulings AN to AQ, and
    #       ROADMAP.md, both modified) are checked against their blobs' hashes,
    #       the base's copies (the close-out's) the controls; its message read
    #       folded, three pairs.
    #  E68. The crate commit's six files (package.rs, chart.rs, chart_write.rs,
    #       package/tests.rs and tests/chart.rs modified, tests/chartsheet.rs
    #       added) and the tool commit's two (main.rs and tests/null_rewrite.rs
    #       modified); the phase diff those eight and the doc commit's three
    #       alone; the tree outside them the base's; no manifest and no lock;
    #       nothing under scripts/; CHANGELOG.md, README.md and
    #       scripts/passthrough-run.sh not in the range; the crate's messages
    #       the base's and the part's three ("Errors"), none gone; and no line
    #       of this script outside a comment naming the ops test, the
    #       comparison record's test or the container tool.
    #  G68. The two files the phase adds, specs/0038 and tests/chartsheet.rs,
    #       not executable; every modified file keeps its base mode.
    #  H68. No Python script is in the phase diff; the calls per file of the
    #       PYB reader at the phase commit, read when the generator ran by the
    #       same reader, are part 6 P3's.
    #  I68. The four test files in the phase diff; the one base test the spec
    #       supersedes (tests/chart.rs's
    #       a_chartsheet_part_is_refused_as_not_a_worksheet: specs/0038
    #       "Reading" reads a chartsheet through its drawing element, which
    #       specs/0034 P1 ruling B refused) gone, renamed, and no other base
    #       test gone or edited; the test names of the whole tree the base's
    #       less that one and the new tests derived from the diff (the renamed
    #       chart test, the chartsheet tests, the tool's two), as FACTS.md
    #       names them; the chartsheet tests counted, one gated to images and
    #       one to comments.
    #  J68. The status patterns admit this phase's logs under
    #       records/logs/m6p1-p1/ (this log runs before the run log writes its
    #       records), and specs/0038 modified and uncommitted only when
    #       PLANNING_SPEC is given and the working tree's copy hashes to it
    #       (planning's rulings A to C), its sha256 printed, with two controls.
    #  K64. The consumer crate also names add_chartsheet.
    #  L63. The public first lines: the base's and one pub fn added,
    #       add_chartsheet under the charts cfg line; none lost, no cfg line
    #       moved; the items under the charts cfg line charts, add_chart,
    #       remove_chart and add_chartsheet.
    #  M68. The crate's feature sets gain charts with comments (cc) for clippy,
    #       doc and tests; each total but cc's is part 6 P4's committed
    #       figure (records/logs/m5p6-p4/test.log at the base) plus the
    #       chartsheet tests the set compiles (ch and cr all but the two gated,
    #       ci and cc all but one, every feature all; the renamed chart test
    #       nets none); cc's total, which no committed log gives, is printed,
    #       checked equal on both targets and above charts alone; the
    #       chartsheet module's tests counted in each run.
    #  Q68. The charts probe's figure printed against part 6's size record's
    #       (records/size-m5p6-2026-10-18-ef9da37.md read at the base, its
    #       verdicts row), checked equal to part 6 P3's committed gate.log's
    #       figure, with the difference; told to planning at any value and
    #       judged nowhere here (part 5 P1's form, a part's first phase; P3's
    #       size log judges this part's cost).
    #  S68. Rulings S, AD and AK a: for every script the phase generates (the
    #       gate, test, negative, run and scan scripts beside this one), the
    #       ruling S line and its planted control, the line "yn, ynnot, is and
    #       isnot, where it calls them, are defined in it" and its control (a
    #       planted script calling yn and is and defining neither), and ruling
    #       AK a's control (a planted is call of an undefined helper), five
    #       lines a script; by an embedded program equivalent to the
    #       generators' gen/defined.py, printed in the log.
    #  V68. Every file of the phase's generator directory hashed, with its
    #       bytes; gen/defined.py checked at the hash the generators pin.
    #  W68. Ruling AK b: each file RERUN_FILES names is counted, hashed with the
    #       directory it is kept in, and a check line asserts every one hashed
    #       and lying in <phase>/superseded, RERUN_FILES naming at least one.
    #  Z68. Ruling Z with AK a: the shape greps' block runs the same program
    #       over this script's own source, its five lines and its planted
    #       controls, and checks the count of names it calls through yn,
    #       ynnot, is and isnot is not 0.
    # CARRIED TO M6 part 1 P2 from M6 part 1 P1's gate script (at the phase
    # commit f917618, printed in records/logs/m6p1-p1/gate.log at P1's record
    # commit, the base; its change list A68 to Z68 is above), generated by
    # <home>/.cache/emend-build/m6p1-p2/gen/gate.py, which checks the template
    # byte-equal to that print and its sha256 against the pin, asserts every
    # replacement's count, and greps for P1's names, subjects, hashes and roles
    # over the header's paragraph and usage lines and the whole body. What
    # differs, and why:
    #  A69. The phase is Milestone 6 part 1 P2 (specs/0038-chartsheets.md, "P2:
    #       the openpyxl fixture and the catalogue"): the log directory
    #       records/logs/m6p1-p2. The base is P1's record commit ("M6 part 1 P1
    #       record:", f948fd9); the phase holds four commits found by their full
    #       subjects, each beginning "M6 part 1 P2: ": the doc commit, then the
    #       carry commit (planning's P1 verification ruling E), then the op
    #       commit, then the limits commit, the phase commit, each the next
    #       one's parent and the doc commit's parent the base. P1's tool commit
    #       named as the phase commit, P1's crate commit named as the carry
    #       commit, and each neighbouring pair of P2's roles swapped, are
    #       refused by the order check (controls). The record-commit control of
    #       commit_class names the base; the code-commit control names the
    #       commit that last modified scripts/openpyxl-ops.py, the op commit.
    #       The count rule reads all four messages; the doc commit states no
    #       count and the three code messages carry the diff-alone sentence.
    #  C69. No prune: P1's prune block, its PRUNE_PREVIOUS input and its five
    #       directories go (P1's gate log removed Milestone 5 part 6's target
    #       roots, rm -r exit 0 where they were there). This phase's own target
    #       root, <phase>/target, is the one it builds into.
    #  D69. The spec is specs/0038, pinned by the hash of its COMMITTED blob at
    #       P2's doc commit (0681b458..., read from the blob when the generator
    #       ran and checked against the brief's full hash) at the phase commit;
    #       the working tree's copy, planning's (ruling J), printed and, when
    #       PLANNING_SPEC is given, checked equal to it; specs/0038 at the base
    #       (P1's doc commit's blob, abe3f167...) and specs/0037 at the phase
    #       commit (P1's doc commit's blob, 186b1740..., unchanged since) are
    #       controls the pin refuses. The doc commit's two files (specs/0038
    #       with the P1 verification and rulings D to I, and ROADMAP.md with
    #       P1's row done, both modified) are checked against their blobs'
    #       hashes, the base's copies (P1's) the controls; its message read
    #       folded, two pairs.
    #  E69. The carry commit's one file (tests/chartsheet.rs), the op commit's
    #       two (scripts/openpyxl-ops.py and scripts/openpyxl-ops-test.sh) and
    #       the limits commit's one (package.rs), each modified; the phase diff
    #       those four and the doc commit's two alone; the tree outside them
    #       the base's; no manifest and no lock; two Rust sources, both under
    #       crates/emend/src, nothing under tools/; the files under scripts/ in
    #       the range the op commit's two; CHANGELOG.md, README.md and
    #       scripts/passthrough-run.sh not in the range; the crate's messages
    #       the base's, none added and none gone (P1 verification ruling G: the
    #       limits alone in this phase); and no line of this script outside a
    #       comment naming the ops test, the comparison record's test or the
    #       container tool (the ops test's path is built from pieces).
    #  G69. The phase adds no file; every modified file keeps its base mode,
    #       the ops test's 100755 among them.
    #  H69. One Python script is in the phase diff, scripts/openpyxl-ops.py,
    #       compiled with every Python script under scripts/; the calls per
    #       file of the PYB reader at the phase commit, read when the generator
    #       ran by the same reader, are P1's: the op adds Python code and a
    #       test line, no Python call.
    #  I69. One test file in the phase diff, tests/chartsheet.rs: its base
    #       tests read, none gone and none edited; the one new test derived
    #       from the diff, a_chartsheet_renamed_and_moved_in_the_write_that_
    #       added_it_is_written_so (ruling E), gated by no cfg line of its own;
    #       the test names of the whole tree the base's and that one; the
    #       chartsheet tests counted, one gated to images and one to comments.
    #       PKG_TESTS, set in P1's cut block and read by the gating check
    #       below it, is set here (the first dry gate's unbound variable).
    #  J69. The status patterns admit this phase's logs under
    #       records/logs/m6p1-p2/ (this log runs before the run log writes its
    #       records), specs/0038 modified and uncommitted only when
    #       PLANNING_SPEC is given and the working tree's copy hashes to it, and
    #       ROADMAP.md modified and uncommitted only when the working tree's
    #       copy hashes to planning's working ROADMAP (b0fd10df..., ruling J's
    #       parity item), each sha256 printed, with controls.
    #  K65. The consumer crate unchanged: what it names, add_chartsheet among
    #       it, is the feature's since P1, none added since.
    #  L64. The public first lines: the base's line for line, none added, none
    #       lost, no cfg line moved; the items under the charts cfg line
    #       charts, add_chart, remove_chart and add_chartsheet, as P1 left them.
    #  M69. Each set's total is P1's committed figure plus the one new
    #       chartsheet test where the set compiles it (charts on: ch, ci, cc, cr
    #       and every feature one more; off and im none): the figures of off,
    #       ch, ci, cc, cr and every feature read from records/logs/m6p1-p1/
    #       test.log at the base, its host line checked equal to its i686 line;
    #       im, which P1's test log does not run, from P1's committed gate.log,
    #       its host line checked equal to its i686 line, and P1's gate.log
    #       figures of the six other sets checked equal to its test.log's; cc
    #       now judged as the others (P1's logs give its figure, 679).
    #  Q69. The charts probe's figure printed against P1's committed gate.log
    #       figure (records/logs/m6p1-p1/gate.log at the base), with the
    #       difference, and against part 6's size record's, part 4's and part 5
    #       P4's as P1 printed them; told to planning at any value and judged
    #       nowhere here (P3's size log judges this part's cost).
    #  S69. Rulings S, AD and AK a over the five scripts of this phase, beside
    #       this one, m6p1-p2-gate.sh to m6p1-p2-scan.sh.
    #  W69. Ruling F (planning's P1 verification): when RERUN_NOTE is set, a
    #       check line that RERUN_FILES names the superseded script that wrote
    #       the attempt, a file ending .sh in <phase>/superseded, with a planted
    #       control, beside ruling AK b's check.
    #  Z69. Ruling D (planning's P1 verification): the shape greps' block runs
    #       the scan's placeholder rule over this script's own source (every
    #       lower-case angle-bracketed token before a slash, and every scrub
    #       target, declared by a "root: <token> stands for" line) and prints
    #       the tokens printed, those declared and the count undeclared, with
    #       two planted controls (an undeclared token found, the same token
    #       declared accepted), the controls' tokens built from pieces.
    set -uo pipefail
    SELF_DIR=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
    SELF=$SELF_DIR/$(basename "$0")
    PHASE_DIR=$(dirname "$SELF_DIR")
    # specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7: this script's own standard error, kept and counted at the end.
    mkdir -p "$PHASE_DIR/out" || exit 2
    ERR=$PHASE_DIR/out/gate.stderr
    exec 2>"$ERR"
    cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || exit 2
    MAIN=$(pwd)
    # Header change 1, kept: the build directory is on the disk, not in /tmp; a
    # dry run names another with GATE_BUILD_ROOT (changes A35 and A36).
    BUILD_ROOT=${GATE_BUILD_ROOT:-$PHASE_DIR/gate}
    # Change J35 (b) and K35: the phase's target root (ruling 8), one
    # subdirectory per source tree (ruling 10 of P3): <repo>'s builds in main,
    # the consumer crate's in consumer.
    SHARED_TARGET=$PHASE_DIR/target
    MAIN_TARGET=$SHARED_TARGET/main
    CONSUMER_TARGET=$SHARED_TARGET/consumer
    mkdir -p "$BUILD_ROOT" "$MAIN_TARGET" "$CONSUMER_TARGET" || exit 2
    export CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$MAIN_TARGET
    # Change N50 (P1's N47, kept): every cargo command this script runs,
    # check.sh's among them, runs offline; nothing this log runs needs the
    # network. Change P50 (P3's P49, kept): Python writes no bytecode cache
    # (specs/0032 "Planning verification and rulings on P2", ruling 6).
    export CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true
    export PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1
    # Change C69: no prune and no PRUNE_PREVIOUS input; P1's gate log pruned
    # Milestone 5 part 6's target roots.
    # Change D66, kept (D67 to D69): planning's working copy's hash, optional
    # (P2's scan form), read from the environment at run time.
    PLANNING_SPEC=${PLANNING_SPEC:-}
    # Change J69: planning's working ROADMAP.md (ruling J's parity item), the
    # hash its message gave, admitted modified in the status below at it alone.
    PLANNING_ROADMAP=b0fd10df3599e27f234f4c6cb9764a26329d92861919f14b98dc25833159833e
    # Change S69 (rulings S, AD and AK a): the scripts this phase generates,
    # beside this one; the gate refuses to start if one is missing.
    GEN_SCRIPTS=""
    for s in gate test negative run scan; do
      [ -f "$SELF_DIR/m6p1-p2-$s.sh" ] || { echo "the phase's $s script is not beside this one" >&2; exit 2; }
      GEN_SCRIPTS="$GEN_SCRIPTS $SELF_DIR/m6p1-p2-$s.sh"
    done
    # Change V68 (planning's P1 brief: every generator file hashed in the
    # gate log): the phase's generator directory, beside the scripts one.
    GEN_DIR=$PHASE_DIR/gen
    [ -d "$GEN_DIR" ] || { echo "the phase's generator directory is not beside this script's" >&2; exit 2; }
    # Change F51, kept: no self-test input.
    # Change T50 (specs/0032 "Planning rulings on the close-out", ruling 1):
    # TMPDIR is <scratch>/tmp, <scratch> standing for <phase>, made if
    # missing and exported before any mktemp, so every mktemp -d without a
    # template, and every tool that honours TMPDIR, writes under it.
    TMPDIR=$PHASE_DIR/tmp
    export TMPDIR
    mkdir -p "$TMPDIR" || exit 2
    # Change S50: the interpreter's name, built so that this text holds no
    # Python call but the ones it makes.
    PY=python
    PYW=${PY}3
    W=$(mktemp -d)
    # Change A24 (A22's form): the consumer crate's directory is made by this
    # run and removed by it at the end. Change T51 (specs/0033 P1 ruling 12):
    # it lies under TMPDIR, as every mktemp call of this script does; the
    # scrub writes it <scratch>/consumer.
    CONSUMER=$(mktemp -d "$TMPDIR/consumer.XXXXXX") || exit 2
    # Every printed line passes through this scrub: the repository first, since it
    # lies inside the home directory, then the deeper directories before the
    # shallower ones.
    scrub() {
      sed -E -e "s#$MAIN([^-A-Za-z0-9_.]|\$)#<repo>\\1#g" -e "s#$W#<scratch>/w#g" -e "s#$CONSUMER#<scratch>/consumer#g" -e "s#$TMPDIR#<scratch>/tmp#g" -e "s#$SHARED_TARGET#<target>#g" -e "s#$BUILD_ROOT#<build>#g" \
        -e "s#$SELF_DIR#<scripts>#g" -e "s#$PHASE_DIR#<phase>#g" -e "s#$HOME#<home>#g"
    }
    exec 3>&1
    exec > >(scrub >&3)
    SCRUB_PID=$!
    C=${1:?phase commit required}
    # Change C3: the library at the phase commit, not the main tree's file.
    git show "$C:scripts/counting.sh" >"$W/counting.sh" || exit 2
    . "$W/counting.sh"
    
    trap 'rm -rf "${W:?}" "${CONSUMER:?}"' EXIT INT TERM HUP
    fail=0
    note() { echo "  $1: $2"; [ "$2" = as-expected ] || fail=1; }
    is() { if "$@"; then echo as-expected; else echo UNEXPECTED; fi; }
    # For a control that must FAIL. `is ! f x` cannot express it: `!` is a shell
    # keyword, so "$@" would try to run a command named "!" and the helper would
    # print UNEXPECTED whatever f did.
    isnot() { if "$@"; then echo UNEXPECTED; else echo as-expected; fi; }
    ctl() {
      local label=$1 want=$2 n r
      shift 2
      n=$("$@")
      r=$([ "$n" = "$want" ] && echo as-expected || echo UNEXPECTED)
      echo "  control $label count=$n expected=$want: $r"
      [ "$r" = as-expected ] || fail=1
    }
    
    # specs/0001-bootstrap.md, "Rules for every phase" (M1 part 3 P1 ruling 11):
    # the phase commit is GIVEN to the script and compared with git rev-parse
    # HEAD before the run, with a control that refuses a fabricated commit.
    HEAD_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD)
    NULL_SHA=0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
    full_sha() {
      printf '%s' "$1" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE '^[0-9a-f]{40}$'
    }
    is_head() { full_sha "$1" && [ "$1" = "$HEAD_SHA" ]; }
    not_head() { if is_head "$1"; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    echo "command: git rev-parse HEAD"
    echo "invocation: bash <scripts>/$(basename "$0") $*"
    echo "about: M6 part 1 P2 phase commits, last is $C; HEAD at run: $HEAD_SHA"
    # Change J4: every environment input this script reads, on a labelled line.
    echo "input (environment) RERUN_NOTE: $([ -n "${RERUN_NOTE:-}" ] && echo "set; its text is the rerun line below" || echo unset)"
    echo "input (environment) RERUN_FILES: ${RERUN_FILES:-unset}"
    echo "input (environment) ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: $([ -n "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ] && echo set || echo unset)"
    echo "input (environment) HOME: $([ -n "${HOME:-}" ] && echo "set; the scrub writes it <home>" || echo unset)"
    echo "input (set by this script) CARGO_NET_OFFLINE: $CARGO_NET_OFFLINE (exported before any cargo command; every cargo command of this log runs offline; changes N46 to N50)"
    echo "input (set by this script) PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE: $PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE (exported before any Python runs, so none writes a bytecode cache; changes P49 and P50)"
    echo "input: TMPDIR=$TMPDIR (set by this script, made if missing and exported before any mktemp; specs/0032 \"Planning rulings on the close-out (2026-10-12)\", ruling 1; change T50)"
    echo "input: no PRUNE_PREVIOUS: this phase prunes nothing, P1's gate log having pruned Milestone 5 part 6's target roots (change C69)"
    echo "input (environment) PLANNING_SPEC: ${PLANNING_SPEC:-unset} (planning's working copy's hash; changes D66 to D69)"
    echo "input (set by this script) PLANNING_ROADMAP: $PLANNING_ROADMAP (planning's working ROADMAP.md's hash, as ruling J's message gave it; change J69)"
    echo "input: no self-test input: this phase changes no gate file (change F51, kept)"
    echo "input (environment) GATE_BUILD_ROOT: ${GATE_BUILD_ROOT:+set; the build directory below is it}${GATE_BUILD_ROOT:-unset, so <build> is <phase>/gate}"
    echo
    echo "## the environment line: the root's mount propagation and the keepalive (specs/0029 item 1 and \"Planning rulings during P1\", ruling 4 (i); specs/0001 \"Rules for every phase\"; change B37)"
    # podman's first call warns on the script's standard error when the root is
    # not a shared mount, and a session's logged runs die with an ssh drop unless
    # the host's scheduled task holds its keepalive; both are read here, before
    # anything else the log does, each checked with controls on the same check.
    echo "command: findmnt -no PROPAGATION /"
    PROPAGATION=$(findmnt -no PROPAGATION / </dev/null)
    echo "  the root's mount propagation: ${PROPAGATION:-nothing read}"
    prop_shared() { [ "$1" = shared ]; }
    note "the root's mount propagation is shared" "$(is prop_shared "$PROPAGATION")"
    note "control: the same check refuses private" "$(isnot prop_shared private)"
    note "control: the same check refuses an empty reading" "$(isnot prop_shared "")"
    # keep_count <pattern>: the processes whose whole command line matches the
    # anchored pattern, or "error" when pgrep fails (exit 2 or more); pgrep -c
    # prints 0 and exits 1 when none match.
    keep_count() {
      local n rc
      n=$(pgrep -fc -- "$1" </dev/null)
      rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -le 1 ]; then printf '%s' "$n"; else printf error; fi
    }
    KEEPALIVE_RE='^/usr/bin/sleep 2147483647$'
    echo "command: pgrep -fc -- '$KEEPALIVE_RE'"
    NKEEP=$(keep_count "$KEEPALIVE_RE")
    echo "  keepalive processes: $NKEEP"
    keep_one() { [ "$1" = 1 ]; }
    note "exactly one keepalive process is running" "$(is keep_one "$NKEEP")"
    note "control: the same check refuses 0" "$(isnot keep_one 0)"
    note "control: the same check refuses 2" "$(isnot keep_one 2)"
    note "control: the same check refuses error" "$(isnot keep_one error)"
    # The counting form on a process this script starts: a sleep with a duration
    # no other process uses, disowned so that its end prints no job notice on the
    # script's standard error, counted while it runs and after it is stopped.
    CTL_RE='^sleep 987654321$'
    sleep 987654321 </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &
    CTL_PID=$!
    disown "$CTL_PID"
    k=0
    while [ "$(keep_count "$CTL_RE")" != 1 ] && [ "$k" -lt 50 ]; do sleep 0.1; k=$((k + 1)); done
    ctl "keep_count on the anchored command line of a sleep this script started" 1 keep_count "$CTL_RE"
    kill "$CTL_PID"
    k=0
    while [ "$(keep_count "$CTL_RE")" != 0 ] && [ "$k" -lt 50 ]; do sleep 0.1; k=$((k + 1)); done
    ctl "keep_count on the same line after this script stopped it" 0 keep_count "$CTL_RE"
    ctl "keep_count on a pattern no process matches" 0 keep_count '^no such command line here$'
    
    echo
    echo "## the four shape greps, over this script, before its log (specs/0001; PYB, change S50), the defined-helper program over this script's own source (rulings Z and AK a; change Z68), and the placeholder rule over it (ruling D; change Z69)"
    # specs/0001: the scan greps every printed script for three forbidden shapes,
    # and they run here first, over this script's own file, each with a positive
    # control through the same command form. The patterns and the fixture are
    # assembled from pieces at run time, so this script's text holds none.
    D='$'
    SHAPE_CHAIN="\\${D}\\((yn|is|isnot) .*(&&|\\|\\|)"
    SHAPE_BANG="\\${D}\\((yn|is|isnot) !"
    SHAPE_ASSIGN="\\${D}\\((yn|is|isnot) [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*="
    SHAPE_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
    printf '%s\n' "x=${D}(is [ 1 ] && true)" "y=${D}(isnot ! true)" "z=${D}(yn N=1 true)" >"$SHAPE_DIR/fixture"
    echo "command: count_matches -E <shape> <this script>, and the same over a fixture holding one line of each shape"
    for shape in CHAIN BANG ASSIGN; do
      var=SHAPE_$shape
      n_self=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$SELF")
      n_fix=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$SHAPE_DIR/fixture")
      echo "  shape $shape: in this script $n_self; in the fixture $n_fix"
      SHAPE_RESULT_SELF=$n_self
      SHAPE_RESULT_FIX=$n_fix
      note "$shape: this script holds none" "$(is [ "$SHAPE_RESULT_SELF" = 0 ])"
      note "control: $shape: the same form finds the fixture's one line" "$(is [ "$SHAPE_RESULT_FIX" = 1 ])"
    done
    # py_calls <file>: one line "<line> <I> <B>" per Python call, I and B 1 when
    # an option before the first non-option argument holds that letter. A call:
    # a line whose first non-blank character is not "#" (a shebang counts as a
    # comment) where the interpreter's name, alone or ending a path, is followed
    # by an argument that begins with "-" (an option, or "-" for standard input)
    # (the name may follow "$(" or "(")
    # or names a .py file. Options are read up to "-", a non-option, or an
    # option holding c or m (the program follows it); a bundled option such as
    # -IB counts each letter. The interpreter's name is PYW, so this text
    # itself holds no call.
    py_calls() {
      awk -v w="$PYW" -v q="'" '
        function unq(s) {
          while (substr(s, 1, 1) ~ /[$("`]/ || substr(s, 1, 1) == q) s = substr(s, 2)
          while (length(s) > 0 && (substr(s, length(s)) ~ /["`)]/ || substr(s, length(s)) == q)) s = substr(s, 1, length(s) - 1)
          return s
        }
        {
          t = $0; sub(/^[[:space:]]+/, "", t)
          if (substr(t, 1, 1) == "#") next
          n = split($0, f, /[[:space:]]+/)
          for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            tok = unq(f[i])
            if (tok != w && tok !~ ("[/(]" w "$")) continue
            if (i + 1 > n) continue
            first = unq(f[i + 1])
            if (first !~ /^-/ && first !~ /[.]py$/) continue
            hi = 0; hb = 0
            for (j = i + 1; j <= n; j++) {
              o = unq(f[j])
              if (o == "-" || o !~ /^-/) break
              if (o ~ /^-[A-Za-z]+$/) {
                if (o ~ /I/) hi = 1
                if (o ~ /B/) hb = 1
                if (o ~ /[cm]/) break
              }
            }
            print NR, hi, hb
          }
        }' "$1"
    }
    # PYB (change S50): a Python call without both -I and -B (specs/0032
    # "Planning verification and rulings on P3", ruling 4), over this
    # script, with a fixture through the same form.
    no_ib() { awk '$2 == 0 || $3 == 0' "$1" | count_lines /dev/stdin; }
    py_calls "$SELF" >"$SHAPE_DIR/self.calls"
    printf '%s\n' "x=\$($PYW -B -c 'y')" "z=\$($PYW -I -B -c 'y')" >"$SHAPE_DIR/pyb-fixture"
    py_calls "$SHAPE_DIR/pyb-fixture" >"$SHAPE_DIR/fix.calls"
    n_self=$(no_ib "$SHAPE_DIR/self.calls")
    n_fix=$(no_ib "$SHAPE_DIR/fix.calls")
    echo "  shape PYB (a Python call without -I -B): in this script $n_self, of its $(count_lines "$SHAPE_DIR/self.calls") calls; in the fixture $n_fix, of its $(count_lines "$SHAPE_DIR/fix.calls")"
    note "PYB: this script holds none" "$(is [ "$n_self" = 0 ])"
    note "PYB: this script makes some calls, each read by the form" "$(is [ "$(count_lines "$SHAPE_DIR/self.calls")" -ge 1 ])"
    note "control: PYB: the same form finds the fixture's one line of two" "$(is [ "$n_fix" = 1 ])"
    # TMPDIR (change T50): this run's mktemp -d directories under it.
    under() { case "$1/" in "$2"/*) return 0 ;; esac; return 1; }
    echo "command: printenv TMPDIR -> $(printenv TMPDIR)"
    tmpdir_set() { [ "$(printenv TMPDIR)" = "$PHASE_DIR/tmp" ]; }
    note "TMPDIR is exported as <scratch>/tmp (change T50)" "$(is tmpdir_set)"
    tmpdir_ctl() { (export TMPDIR=$PHASE_DIR; tmpdir_set); }
    note "control: tmpdir_set refuses TMPDIR set to <phase>" "$(isnot tmpdir_ctl)"
    note "this run's mktemp -d working directory, <scratch>/w, is under TMPDIR (change T50)" "$(is under "$W" "$TMPDIR")"
    note "the shape fixture's mktemp -d directory is under TMPDIR (change T50)" "$(is under "$SHAPE_DIR" "$TMPDIR")"
    note "control: under refuses <phase>, TMPDIR's parent" "$(isnot under "$PHASE_DIR" "$TMPDIR")"
    note "control: under refuses <build>, a directory beside TMPDIR" "$(isnot under "$BUILD_ROOT" "$TMPDIR")"
    # Change T51 (specs/0033 "Planning rulings on P1", ruling 12): every
    # mktemp call this script makes, file or directory, lies under TMPDIR.
    # The calls are read from this script's own text (a non-comment line
    # holding a command substitution of mktemp), each a directory (-d), none
    # a plain file; the three directories they make are each checked under
    # TMPDIR with T50's form and its controls. The fixture's lines name the
    # command through a variable, so this text holds no call but its own.
    MK=mktemp
    MKTEMP_RE='^[^#]*[$][(]'"$MK"'([[:space:]]|[)])'
    mk_calls() { LC_ALL=C "$G" -E "$MKTEMP_RE" "$1" | count_lines /dev/stdin; }
    mk_files() { LC_ALL=C "$G" -E "$MKTEMP_RE" "$1" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -vE "[(]$MK -d([[:space:]]|[)])" | count_lines /dev/stdin; }
    echo "command: the non-comment lines of this script holding a command substitution of mktemp, and of them those without -d"
    LC_ALL=C "$G" -n -E "$MKTEMP_RE" "$SELF" | sed 's/^/    /'
    n_mk=$(mk_calls "$SELF")
    n_mk_files=$(mk_files "$SELF")
    echo "  mktemp calls in this script: $n_mk; of them a plain file: $n_mk_files"
    note "this script makes three mktemp calls, <scratch>/w, the shape fixture's and <scratch>/consumer (change T51)" "$(is [ "$n_mk" = 3 ])"
    note "none of them makes a plain file (change T51)" "$(is [ "$n_mk_files" = 0 ])"
    printf '%s\n' "a=\$($MK -d)" "b=\$($MK)" "# c=\$($MK -d)" "d=\$($MK -d \"\$X/y.XXXXXX\")" "e=$MK" >"$SHAPE_DIR/mk-fixture"
    ctl "mktemp calls in a planted fixture of five lines (three calls, a comment, a bare name), same command form" 3 mk_calls "$SHAPE_DIR/mk-fixture"
    ctl "of them a plain file, same command form" 1 mk_files "$SHAPE_DIR/mk-fixture"
    note "<scratch>/consumer, the consumer crate's mktemp -d directory, is under TMPDIR (change T51)" "$(is under "$CONSUMER" "$TMPDIR")"
    consumer_ctl() { local CONSUMER=$BUILD_ROOT/consumer.x; under "$CONSUMER" "$TMPDIR"; }
    note "control: under refuses the directory P1 used, under <build>" "$(isnot consumer_ctl)"
    # Change Z68 (ruling Z, specs/0037 "Planning verification of P3", and
    # ruling AK a, carried by specs/0038 "Generator rules"): the defined-helper
    # program, the generators' gen/defined.py as this log's own program,
    # written here and run over this script's own source, its five lines and
    # its planted controls; the section below prints it and runs it over every
    # script the phase generates (rulings S, AD and AK a). A call's name is the
    # word after "$(yn ", "$(ynnot ", "$(is " or "$(isnot " outside a comment
    # line; it is defined when a line of the script reads "<name>() {"; the
    # commands the scripts call by name through those four are not helpers.
    # Ruling AD, widened by AK a: yn, ynnot, is and isnot, where a script calls
    # them, are defined in it. The planted calls are built from pieces, so
    # this text holds no call of an undefined name.
    DC_WORD=pod; DC_WORD=${DC_WORD}man
    cat >"$W/defined.py" <<'DEFINED'
    import re, sys
    CALL = re.compile(r"\$\((?:yn|ynnot|is|isnot) ([^ )\"]+)")
    WRAP = re.compile(r"\$\((yn|ynnot|is|isnot) ")
    DEF = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\(\) \{", re.M)
    
    
    def calls(script, pattern):
        called = set()
        for line in script.split("\n"):
            if line.lstrip().startswith("#"):
                continue
            called.update(pattern.findall(line))
        return called
    
    
    def undefined(script, commands):
        called = calls(script, CALL)
        defined = set(DEF.findall(script))
        return sorted(n for n in called if n not in commands and n not in defined), len(called)
    
    
    def wrappers_undefined(script):
        called = calls(script, WRAP)
        defined = set(DEF.findall(script))
        return sorted(w for w in called if w not in defined), sorted(called)
    
    
    commands = sys.argv[1].split()
    plant = "zz_planted_" + "undefined_helper"
    iplant = "zz_planted_" + "is_helper"
    for path in sys.argv[2:]:
        name = path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
        text = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
        missing, n = undefined(text, commands)
        print("%s: every helper a yn, ynnot, is or isnot call names is defined in it (%d names called; the commands %s are not helpers): %s"
              % (name, n, " ".join(commands), "yes" if not missing else "no, undefined: " + " ".join(missing)))
        planted = text + '\ncheck "planted" "' + "$" + "(yn " + plant + ')"\n'
        pmissing, _ = undefined(planted, commands)
        ok = (not missing) and pmissing == [plant]
        print("%s: control: the same check finds a planted call of an undefined helper: %s" % (name, "yes" if ok else "no"))
        wmissing, wcalled = wrappers_undefined(text)
        print("%s: yn, ynnot, is and isnot, where it calls them, are defined in it (it calls %s): %s"
              % (name, " and ".join(wcalled) if wcalled else "neither",
                 "yes" if not wmissing else "no, undefined: " + " ".join(wmissing)))
        cmissing, _ = wrappers_undefined('x="' + "$" + '(yn true)"\ny="' + "$" + '(is true)"\n')
        print("%s: control: the same check finds yn and is undefined in a planted script calling both and defining neither: %s"
              % (name, "yes" if cmissing == ["is", "yn"] else "no"))
        imissing, _ = undefined(text + '\ncheck "planted" "' + "$" + "(is " + iplant + ')"\n', commands)
        iok = (not missing) and imissing == [iplant]
        print("%s: control: the same check finds a planted is call of an undefined helper: %s" % (name, "yes" if iok else "no"))
    DEFINED
    echo "command: python3 -I -B <scratch>/w/defined.py '<the commands>' <this script> (change Z68; rulings Z and AK a)"
    python3 -I -B "$W/defined.py" "[ cmp true $DC_WORD" "$SELF" >"$W/self-defined.out" 2>"$W/self-defined.err"
    self_def_rc=$?
    sed 's/^/  /' "$W/self-defined.out"
    echo "  exit $self_def_rc; stderr bytes $(wc -c <"$W/self-defined.err")"
    n_self_yes=$(count_matches -E ': yes$' "$W/self-defined.out")
    self_def_ok() { [ "$self_def_rc" = 0 ] && [ ! -s "$W/self-defined.err" ] && [ "$n_self_yes" = 5 ] && [ "$(count_lines "$W/self-defined.out")" = 5 ]; }
    note "this script's own source defines every helper its yn, ynnot, is and isnot calls name and the four where it calls them, and its three planted controls find their names (5 lines yes; change Z68, rulings Z and AK a)" "$(is self_def_ok)"
    self_count_ok() { [ "$(count_matches -E ': every helper .* [(]0 names called;' "$W/self-defined.out")" = 0 ]; }
    note "this script's own count of names called through yn, ynnot, is and isnot is not 0 (ruling AK a; change Z68)" "$(is self_count_ok)"
    printf '%s\n' 'f() { :; }' "x=\$""(yn f)" "y=\$""(ynnot g)" "z=\$""(is h)" >"$W/c-defined.sh"
    python3 -I -B "$W/defined.py" "[ cmp true $DC_WORD" "$W/c-defined.sh" >"$W/c-defined.out" 2>&1
    echo "  over a planted script defining f alone and calling f, g and h through yn, ynnot and is:"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$W/c-defined.out"
    c_def_ok() { [ "$(count_matches -F 'no, undefined: g h' "$W/c-defined.out")" = 1 ] && [ "$(count_matches -F 'no, undefined: is yn ynnot' "$W/c-defined.out")" = 1 ]; }
    note "control: the same program names g and h undefined, and is, yn and ynnot undefined, in the planted script (changes Z68 and S68; rulings Z, S, AD and AK a)" "$(is c_def_ok)"
    # Change Z69 (ruling D, planning's P1 verification): the scan's placeholder
    # rule over this script's own source, printed in the log it writes. A
    # placeholder is a lower-case angle-bracketed word printed immediately
    # before a slash, or the bracketed word a sed scrub writes; it is
    # declared when a root line (a line beginning "root: ", or an echo of one)
    # says the word stands for something. The controls' tokens are built from
    # pieces, so this text holds none undeclared.
    ph_used() {
      {
        LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -o -E 's#[^#]*#<[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*>' "$1" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -o -E '<[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*>$'
        LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -o -E '<[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*>/' "$1" | sed 's#/$##'
      } | LC_ALL=C sort -u
    }
    ph_declared() {
      LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -E '(^|echo ")root: ' "$1" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -o -E '<[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*>(/[a-z0-9]+)? stands for' |
        LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -o -E '^<[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*>' | LC_ALL=C sort -u
    }
    ph_undeclared() { LC_ALL=C comm -23 <(ph_used "$1") <(ph_declared "$1") | count_lines /dev/stdin; }
    echo "command: the scan's placeholder rule over this script's own source: the tokens printed (before a slash, or a scrub's target), the tokens its root lines declare, and the count of those printed and not declared (change Z69; ruling D)"
    echo "  this script's own source: placeholders printed: $(ph_used "$SELF" | tr '\n' ' '); declared by root lines: $(ph_declared "$SELF" | tr '\n' ' '); undeclared: $(ph_undeclared "$SELF")"
    note "every placeholder this script's own source prints is declared by a root line (change Z69; ruling D)" "$(is [ "$(ph_undeclared "$SELF")" = 0 ])"
    note "this script's own source prints some placeholders, each read by the rule (change Z69)" "$(is [ "$(ph_used "$SELF" | count_lines /dev/stdin)" -ge 1 ])"
    PL='<'
    printf 'echo "root: %srepo> stands for x"\necho "read %srepo>/a and %splanted>/b"\n' "$PL" "$PL" "$PL" >"$SHAPE_DIR/c-ph-bad"
    printf 'echo "root: %srepo> stands for x; %splanted> stands for y"\necho "read %srepo>/a and %splanted>/b"\n' "$PL" "$PL" "$PL" "$PL" >"$SHAPE_DIR/c-ph-ok"
    ctl "the same rule over a planted script printing a token before a slash that no root line declares (change Z69; ruling D)" 1 ph_undeclared "$SHAPE_DIR/c-ph-bad"
    ctl "the same rule over the planted script with that token declared in its root line (change Z69; ruling D)" 0 ph_undeclared "$SHAPE_DIR/c-ph-ok"
    rm -rf "${SHAPE_DIR:?}"
    
    
    echo
    echo "## every helper a yn, ynnot, is or isnot call names is defined in each script this phase generates, and the four where each calls them (specs/0037 \"Planning verification of P2\", ruling S, \"Planning verification of P3\", ruling AD, and ruling AK a, as specs/0038 \"Generator rules\" carries them; changes S66 to S69)"
    # The program written in the shape block above (change Z68), printed here
    # and run over the five scripts the phase generates: per script, ruling
    # S's line and its planted control, ruling AD's line and its control, a
    # planted script calling yn and is and defining neither, and ruling AK a's
    # control, a planted is call of an undefined helper.
    sed 's/^/    /' "$W/defined.py"
    echo "command: python3 -I -B <scratch>/w/defined.py '<the commands>' <scripts>/m6p1-p2-<each>.sh, for the five scripts (change S69)"
    python3 -I -B "$W/defined.py" "[ cmp true $DC_WORD" $GEN_SCRIPTS >"$W/defined.out" 2>"$W/defined.err"
    def_rc=$?
    sed 's/^/  /' "$W/defined.out"
    echo "  exit $def_rc; stderr bytes $(wc -c <"$W/defined.err")"
    n_def_yes=$(count_matches -E ': yes$' "$W/defined.out")
    n_def_no=$(count_matches -E ': no' "$W/defined.out")
    def_all() { [ "$def_rc" = 0 ] && [ ! -s "$W/defined.err" ] && [ "$n_def_yes" = 25 ] && [ "$n_def_no" = 0 ]; }
    note "each of the five scripts defines every helper its yn, ynnot, is and isnot calls name and the four where it calls them, and each script's three planted controls find their names (25 lines yes; changes S66 to S69)" "$(is def_all)"
    printf '%s\n' "x=\$""(yn true)" "y=\$""(is true)" >"$W/c-wrap.sh"
    python3 -I -B "$W/defined.py" "[ cmp true $DC_WORD" "$W/c-wrap.sh" >"$W/c-wrap.out" 2>&1
    echo "  over a planted script calling yn and is and defining none of the four:"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$W/c-wrap.out"
    note "control: the same program names is and yn undefined in that planted script (change S68; rulings AD and AK a)" "$(is [ "$(count_matches -F 'no, undefined: is yn' "$W/c-wrap.out")" = 1 ])"
    
    echo
    echo "## every file of the phase's generator directory, hashed (planning's P1 brief; change V68)"
    # Change V68: each regular file under <phase>/gen, sorted by its path, with
    # its sha256 and bytes; gen/defined.py checked at the hash the generators
    # pin, with a control.
    DEFINED_PIN=ee9f81359e07025bf08825c9562a853c3403115dc9a4f39c554f90533df9ca6d
    echo "command: find <phase>/gen -type f | sort; sha256sum and wc -c of each"
    find "$GEN_DIR" -type f | LC_ALL=C sort >"$W/gen-files"
    while IFS= read -r gf; do
      printf '    %s  %s bytes  <phase>/gen/%s\n' "$(sha256sum <"$gf" | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$(wc -c <"$gf")" "${gf#"$GEN_DIR"/}"
    done <"$W/gen-files"
    echo "  generator files hashed: $(count_lines "$W/gen-files")"
    note "some generator files were hashed (change V68)" "$(is [ "$(count_lines "$W/gen-files")" -ge 1 ])"
    def_pinned() { [ "$(sha256sum <"$GEN_DIR/defined.py" | cut -d' ' -f1)" = "$DEFINED_PIN" ]; }
    note "gen/defined.py is the copy the generators pin, ee9f8135... (change V68; ruling AK a)" "$(is def_pinned)"
    def_pin_ctl() { local GEN_DIR=$W/no-such-gen; def_pinned 2>/dev/null; }
    note "control: the same check refuses a directory without the program (change V68)" "$(isnot def_pin_ctl)"
    
    echo
    echo "## the network: every cargo command of this log runs offline (changes N46 to N50)"
    echo "command: printenv CARGO_NET_OFFLINE -> $(printenv CARGO_NET_OFFLINE)"
    offline_set() { [ "$(printenv CARGO_NET_OFFLINE)" = true ]; }
    note "CARGO_NET_OFFLINE is exported as true, so cargo and check.sh, which inherit it, run offline" "$(is offline_set)"
    offline_ctl() { local CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=false; offline_set; }
    note "control: offline_set refuses CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=false" "$(isnot offline_ctl)"
    offline_ctl2() { (unset CARGO_NET_OFFLINE; offline_set); }
    note "control: offline_set refuses CARGO_NET_OFFLINE unset" "$(isnot offline_ctl2)"
    
    echo
    echo "## Python's bytecode cache: off for every Python this log runs (changes P49 and P50; specs/0032 \"Planning verification and rulings on P2\", ruling 6)"
    echo "command: printenv PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE -> $(printenv PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE)"
    pyc_off() { [ "$(printenv PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE)" = 1 ]; }
    note "PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE is exported as 1, so every Python this log runs, which inherits it, writes no bytecode cache" "$(is pyc_off)"
    pyc_ctl() { (unset PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE; pyc_off); }
    note "control: pyc_off refuses PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE unset" "$(isnot pyc_ctl)"
    pyc_ctl2() { (export PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=; pyc_off); }
    note "control: pyc_off refuses PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE empty" "$(isnot pyc_ctl2)"
    
    echo
    echo "## the spec, pinned by the hash of its committed blob (changes B33, B35 and D37; specs/0018 close-out ruling 4)"
    SPEC=specs/0038-chartsheets.md
    # The doc commit's blob's hash, read from the blob when the generator ran and
    # checked there against the brief's full hash, its first 8 digits the
    # prefix planning's brief gave (changes D63, D66 to D69).
    SPEC_GAVE=0681b458e3cce8910cb071c18248fe979012b6c5fadfb719c0181395eef0b181
    echo "command: git show <phase commit>:$SPEC | sha256sum; sha256sum $SPEC (the working tree's copy, printed only)"
    SPEC_SHA=$(sha256sum <"$SPEC" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    SPEC_HEAD=$(git show "$C:$SPEC" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "  the phase commit's blob: sha256 $SPEC_HEAD"
    echo "  the doc commit's blob's hash, its first 8 digits the prefix planning's brief gave: $SPEC_GAVE"
    echo "  the working tree's copy, planning's when modified (its ruling J, written there after P2's doc commit and uncommitted; change D69), not the pin: sha256 $SPEC_SHA"
    # Change D66, kept (D67 to D69; ruling J): PLANNING_SPEC, when given, is
    # planning's uncommitted copy's hash, and the working tree's copy must hash
    # to it (P2's scan form).
    if [ -n "$PLANNING_SPEC" ]; then
      echo "  PLANNING_SPEC, planning's working copy's hash as given: $PLANNING_SPEC"
      note "the working tree's spec hashes to PLANNING_SPEC (changes D66 to D69)" "$(is [ "$SPEC_SHA" = "$PLANNING_SPEC" ])"
      planning_spec_ctl() { [ "$SPEC_HEAD" = "$PLANNING_SPEC" ]; }
      note "control: the same comparison refuses the phase commit's blob against PLANNING_SPEC (changes D66 to D69)" "$(isnot planning_spec_ctl)"
    else
      echo "  PLANNING_SPEC is not set: the working tree's copy is printed only"
    fi
    echo "command: sha256sum ROADMAP.md (the working tree's copy, planning's, printed here and checked in the status below; change J69)"
    echo "  the working tree's ROADMAP.md: sha256 $(sha256sum <ROADMAP.md | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    note "the spec at the phase commit is the doc commit's blob" "$(is [ "$SPEC_HEAD" = "$SPEC_GAVE" ])"
    prefix_ok() { [ "${SPEC_HEAD:0:8}" = 0681b458 ]; }
    note "its first 8 digits are the prefix planning's brief gave (0681b458)" "$(is prefix_ok)"
    prefix_ctl() { local SPEC_HEAD=$P5_SPEC_CTL; prefix_ok; }
